  "worked_solution": "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{7(4x-5)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the critical point $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(0) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 0-5)}{(2\\cdot {0}^{2}-5\\cdot 0-3)^{2}}=\\frac{35}{9}>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 2-5)}{(2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3)^{2}}=-\\frac{21}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4})$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $(\\frac{5}{4},3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n* At $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{4}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has $1$ local maximum and $0$ local minima on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$",
